            <text class="fm-t" x="615" y="541"><tspan class="fm-n">13</tspan>  id_token · access_token · refresh_token</text>
            <line class="fm-m r" x1="446" y1="582" x2="124" y2="582" />
            <text class="fm-t" x="285" y="575"><tspan class="fm-n">14</tspan>  Set-Cookie · 302 → /home/secure ✓</text>
        </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="legend">
        <span>request</span>
        <span class="r">response or redirect</span>
        <span class="op">done by hand, not by the protocol</span>
    </div>

    <div class="scroll-x" style="margin-top:16px">
        <table>
            <tr><th>#</th><th>What happens</th><th>OAuth 2 / OIDC reference</th><th>On this page</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td>1–2</td>
                <td>This app authenticates <em>as itself</em> and is given a token allowed to register clients.</td>
                <td>Client credentials grant — RFC 6749 §4.4; initial access token — RFC 7591 §3</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-1">Step 1</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>3–4</td>
                <td>The client's metadata goes to the registration endpoint; the IdP answers with its identity and one-shot credentials.</td>
                <td>Client registration request / response — RFC 7591 §3.1, §3.2</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-2">Step 2</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>5</td>
                <td>A user is granted access to the new client, with the claims they will carry.</td>
                <td>No OAuth equivalent — Ark's per-user, per-client authorization</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-3">Step 3</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>6</td>
                <td>This app is told to sign in as the new client.</td>
                <td>Client configuration — only <code>Authority</code> and <code>ClientId</code> are required</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-4">Step 4</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>7–8</td>
                <td><code>[Authorize]</code> challenges; the OIDC handler builds the authorization request.</td>
                <td>Authorization request — RFC 6749 §4.1.1; <code>code_challenge</code> — RFC 7636 §4.3; <code>nonce</code> — OIDC Core §3.1.2.1</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-5">Step 5</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>9–11</td>
                <td>The user authenticates at the IdP and is sent back with a code.</td>
                <td>Authorization response — RFC 6749 §4.1.2; <code>state</code> check — §10.12</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-5">Step 5</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>12–13</td>
                <td>The code is redeemed with the PKCE verifier; the ID token is validated against the IdP's published keys.</td>
                <td>Token request — RFC 6749 §4.1.3; <code>code_verifier</code> — RFC 7636 §4.5; ID token validation — OIDC Core §3.1.3.7</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-5">Step 5</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>14</td>
                <td>A session cookie is written and the original request is replayed.</td>
                <td>Not OAuth — the application's own session</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-5">Step 5</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>—</td>
                <td>Later: read or delete the registration with the registration access token.</td>
                <td>Client configuration endpoint — RFC 7592 §2.1, §2.3</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-6">Step 6</a></td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="prerequisites">Before you start — prerequisites</h2>
<div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
        The first three are read live. The last three cannot be seen from here; on a provider
        created from scratch they are already true.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
        <table>
            <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Status</th><th>If it is not met</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The IdP is running and its discovery document is readable at <code>@Model.Authority</code></td>
                <td>
                    @if (Model.DiscoveryOk) { <span class="pill ok">ok</span> }
                    else { <span class="pill bad">no</span> }
                </td>
                <td>
                    From the solution root: <code>dotnet run --project Ark.oAuth.Oidc.Host</code>.
                    The <a href="/">Setup</a> page diagnoses a wrong port, tenant or certificate.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Dynamic registration is switched on (<code>registration_endpoint</code> is advertised)</td>
                <td>
                    @if (Model.SupportsDynamicRegistration) { <span class="pill ok">ok</span> }
                    else if (Model.DiscoveryOk) { <span class="pill bad">off</span> }
                    else { <span class="pill warn">unknown</span> }
                </td>
                <td>
                    Set <code>ark_oauth_server:Oidc:EnableDynamicRegistration</code> to
                    <code>true</code> on the IdP and restart it. Leave
                    <code>RequireRegistrationAccessToken</code> on.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>This app has a working secret for the machine client <code>@Model.MachineClientId</code></td>
                <td>
                    @if (secretWorks) { <span class="pill ok">ok</span> }
                    else if (Model.MachineSecretStatus == "rejected") { <span class="pill bad">rejected</span> }
                    else if (Model.MachineSecretStatus == "unchecked") { <span class="pill warn">unknown</span> }
                    else { <span class="pill bad">not set</span> }
                </td>
                <td>
                    Regenerate it in the <a href="@Model.AdminConsoleUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">admin console</a>
                    and paste it in <a class="step" href="#step-1">Step 1</a> —
                    <a class="step" href="#get-machine-secret">how</a>.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>@Model.MachineClientId</code> is registered for the <code>client_credentials</code> grant and the <code>client.register</code> scope</td>
                <td><span class="pill">check</span></td>
                <td>
                    The IdP seeds it that way. If Step 1 answers <code>invalid_scope</code> or
                    <code>unauthorized_client</code>, add the missing grant or scope to the client
                    record under <strong>Clients</strong>.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>You can sign in to the admin console at <code>@Model.AdminConsoleUrl</code></td>
                <td><span class="pill">check</span></td>
                <td>
                    The first run creates <code>admin</code> with the password set in
                    <code>ark_oauth_server:AdminUser:Password</code>. It is needed once, to get the
                    machine secret; Steps 2 and 3 then run from this page.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>A user account to sign in to the new client with</td>
                <td><span class="pill">check</span></td>
                <td>
                    Any account in the tenant, the admin included. Name a new one in
                    <a class="step" href="#step-3">Step 3</a> and it is created there, with the
                    IdP's default password.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="step-1">Step 1 — Get an initial access token <span class="pill">RFC 6749 §4.4</span></h2>
<div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
        Messages 1–2. This app posts the machine client's own credentials to the token endpoint and
        asks for <code>client.register</code>. No user is involved: the token says "this service
        may create clients", nothing more. You do not press anything for this step — it runs every
        time a form on this page is submitted. On Ark the same token also drives the management
        API, which is what lets this page check the registration and grant access in Step 3.
    </p>
    <div class="kv">
        <div>machine client_id</div><div><code>@Model.MachineClientId</code></div>
        <div>machine client_secret</div>
        <div class="secret-field">
            @* No form of its own: every form on the page copies it in when submitted. *@
            <input type="password" id="machine-secret" value="@Model.MachineSecret"
                   autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false"
                   placeholder="@(secretWorks && !Model.MachineSecretPasted ? "optional — the configured secret works" : "paste the secret from the admin console")" />
            <button class="btn ghost" type="button" id="machine-secret-view">View</button>
        </div>
        <div>client_secret</div>
        <div>
            @switch (Model.MachineSecretStatus)
            {
                case "works":
                    <span class="pill ok">accepted by the IdP</span><span class="secret-src">@secretSource</span>
                    break;
                case "rejected":
                    <span class="pill bad">rejected by the IdP</span><span class="secret-src">@secretSource</span>
                    break;
                case "unchecked":
                    <span class="pill warn">set, not checked — the IdP is unreachable</span>
                    break;
                default:
                    <span class="pill bad">not set</span>
                    break;
            }
        </div>
        <div>scope</div><div><code>client.register</code></div>
        <div>registration_endpoint</div><div><code>@(Model.RegistrationEndpoint ?? "—")</code></div>
    </div>

    @if (!secretWorks)
    {
        <div class="note warn" id="get-machine-secret">
            @if (Model.MachineSecretStatus == "rejected")
            {
                <text><strong>The IdP refused the secret @secretSource.</strong>
                <br />It answered <code>@Model.MachineSecretError</code>. The IdP keeps only a hash of
                the secret, so a value stops working without any sign on this side — most often because
                the IdP's database was recreated (a new database seeds <code>@Model.MachineClientId</code>
                with no secret at all), or because the secret was regenerated since.</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text><strong>This app has no secret for <code>@Model.MachineClientId</code>.</strong>
                <br />Without one, the registration below is sent unauthenticated, and the IdP — which
                requires an initial access token by default — answers <code>invalid_client</code>.</text>
            }
            <ol class="steps" style="margin:10px 0 8px">
                <li>
                    Open the <a href="@Model.AdminConsoleUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">admin console</a>
                    (<code>@Model.AdminConsoleUrl</code>) and sign in as the administrator — the account
                    in <code>ark_oauth_server:AdminUser</code> on the IdP.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Under <strong>Clients</strong>, open <code>@Model.MachineClientId</code> and press
                    <strong>Regenerate secret</strong>. The IdP stores only a hash of it, so this is the one
                    time the value is shown: copy it now.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Paste it into <strong>machine client_secret</strong> above; <strong>View</strong>
                    shows what you pasted. Each form on this page sends it along, and it is kept
                    nowhere — not by this app, not by the IdP.
                </li>
            </ol>
            To reuse it after you leave this page, keep it wherever suits you and hand it to this app
            at start-up — for example <code>export sample__Machine__ClientSecret='…'</code> before
            <code>./run.sh</code>. Not in <code>appsettings.json</code>: that file is in source control.
            If the IdP sets <code>ark_oauth_server:Management:MachineClientSecret</code>, the console will
            not regenerate this client's secret; use that value instead.
        </div>
    }

    @if (Model.InitialAccessToken != null)
    {
        var iat = Model.InitialAccessToken;
        if (iat.Succeeded)
        {
            <div class="note ok">
                <strong>Token obtained.</strong> Scope <code>@(iat.Scope ?? "—")</code>, valid for
                @iat.ExpiresIn seconds. This page asks for a fresh one on every submission, to
                prove the secret still works; service code should call the cached
                <code>GetTokenAsync</code> instead.
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="note bad">
                <strong>Could not obtain an initial access token: @iat.Error</strong><br />
                @iat.ErrorDescription
            </div>
        }
    }

    @if (Model.TargetLookup == "failed")
    {
        <div class="note bad">
            <strong>The secret works, but the management API refused the token.</strong><br />
            <code>@Model.TargetLookupError</code><br />
            It accepts tokens issued to the operator tenant's machine client only, so this app's
            <code>Authority</code> has to name that tenant. Steps 2 and 3 cannot check or change
            anything on the IdP until it does.
        </div>
    }
</div>

<h2 id="step-2">Step 2 — Register the client <span class="pill">RFC 7591 §3.1</span></h2>
<div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
        Messages 3–4. The form is prefilled with <strong>this app's own registration</strong>:
        <code>client_id</code> and <code>client_name</code> are <code>@Model.AppClientId</code>,
        the redirect URIs are this app's callbacks, the scopes are the ones it requests, and it
        asks for the authorization code grant with refresh tokens. Ark honours a requested
        <code>client_id</code> when it is free, so once this succeeds the app can sign in with no
        configuration change. Clear <code>client_id</code> to have the IdP mint one instead.
    </p>

    @if (Model.TargetLookup == "found" && registeredNow == null && target != null)
    {
        <div class="note warn">
            <strong><code>@target.ClientId</code> is already registered on the IdP</strong>
            (@(target.IsConfidential ? target.TokenEndpointAuthMethod : "public client")@(target.IsActive ? "" : ", deactivated")),
            so registering it again answers <code>409</code>. If it is set up the way you want,
            skip to <a class="step" href="#step-3">Step 3</a>. Otherwise remove it and register it
            afresh — RFC 7592's delete in Step 6 needs the registration access token, which is
            gone once the page that showed it is reloaded, so this uses the management API.
            <form method="post" action="/flows/register/remove" data-carry style="margin-top:8px"
                  onsubmit="return confirm('Remove @target.ClientId from the IdP? Its access mappings and refresh tokens go with it.');">
                @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
                @{ CarryFields(includeTarget: true, includeManage: false); }
                <button class="btn ghost" type="submit">Remove @target.ClientId from the IdP</button>
            </form>
        </div>
    }

    <form method="post" action="/flows/register" data-carry>
        @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
        @{ CarryFields(includeTarget: false, includeManage: false); }
        <div class="kv" style="margin-bottom:14px">
            <div>client_id</div>
            <div><input name="RequestedClientId" value="@Model.RequestedClientId" style="width:100%" placeholder="empty: the IdP mints one" /></div>

            <div>client_name</div>
            <div><input name="ClientName" value="@Model.ClientName" style="width:100%" /></div>

            <div>redirect_uris</div>
            <div><input name="RedirectUris" value="@Model.RedirectUris" style="width:100%" /></div>

            <div>post_logout_redirect_uris</div>
            <div><input name="PostLogoutRedirectUris" value="@Model.PostLogoutRedirectUris" style="width:100%" /></div>

            <div>grant_types</div>
            <div><input name="GrantTypes" value="@Model.GrantTypes" style="width:100%" /></div>

            <div>scope</div>
            <div><input name="Scope" value="@Model.Scope" style="width:100%" /></div>

            <div>token_endpoint_auth_method</div>
            <div>
                <select name="TokenEndpointAuthMethod">
                    <option value="none" selected="@(Model.TokenEndpointAuthMethod == "none")">none (public client)</option>
                    <option value="client_secret_post" selected="@(Model.TokenEndpointAuthMethod == "client_secret_post")">client_secret_post</option>
                    <option value="client_secret_basic" selected="@(Model.TokenEndpointAuthMethod == "client_secret_basic")">client_secret_basic</option>
                </select>
            </div>

            <div>application_type</div>
            <div>
                <select name="ApplicationType">
                    <option value="web" selected="@(Model.ApplicationType == "web")">web</option>
                    <option value="native" selected="@(Model.ApplicationType == "native")">native</option>
                </select>
            </div>

        </div>
        <button class="btn" type="submit">Register</button>
    </form>

    <div class="note">
        Redirect URIs are matched exactly and must be absolute. <code>https</code> is required
        except for loopback addresses, which native clients are allowed to use (RFC 8252 §7.3), and
        a fragment is never allowed. Choosing <code>none</code> makes this a public client: no
        secret is issued and PKCE becomes mandatory. This app is currently configured as a
        <strong>@(Model.AppIsConfidential ? "confidential" : "public")</strong> client, so
        <code>@(Model.AppIsConfidential ? "client_secret_post" : "none")</code> is preselected:
        it is the choice that needs no configuration change in Step 4.
    </div>
    <div class="note">
        Every scope must already exist in the IdP's scope catalogue, and the list becomes this
        client's whitelist: a scope it is not registered for is rejected at sign-in, not dropped.
        Keep <code>offline_access</code> if you want a refresh token.
    </div>
</div>

@if (Model.Removal != null)
{
    <h2>Remove result <span class="pill">management API</span></h2>
    <div class="card">
        @if (Model.Removal.Succeeded)
        {
            <div class="note ok">
                <strong><code>@Model.TargetClientId</code> removed from the IdP.</strong>
                Register it again with the form in Step 2. Tokens it was issued stay valid until
                they expire.
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="note bad">
                <strong>@(Model.Removal.StatusCode == 0 ? "Not sent" : Model.Removal.StatusCode.ToString()) — @(Model.Removal.Code ?? "error")</strong><br />
                @Model.Removal.Message
            </div>
        }
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.Removal.RawResponse))
        {
            <pre>@Model.Removal.RawResponse</pre>
        }
    </div>
}

@if (reg != null)
{
    <h2>The registration response <span class="pill">RFC 7591 §3.2</span></h2>
    <div class="card">
        @if (reg.Succeeded)
        {
            <div class="note ok">
                <strong>@reg.StatusCode — client created.</strong>
                @if (isCurrentApp)
                {
                    <text>
                        <code>@reg.ClientId</code> is this app's own client id, so it is now this
                        app's registration. Grant a user access in Step 3 and sign in.
                    </text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text>Steps 3–5 below now name <code>@reg.ClientId</code>, so you can follow them as written.</text>
                }
            </div>
            <div class="kv">
                <div>client_id</div><div><code>@reg.ClientId</code></div>
                <div>client_name</div><div><code>@(reg.ClientName ?? "—")</code></div>
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(reg.ClientSecret))
                {
                    <div>client_secret</div><div><code>@reg.ClientSecret</code></div>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(reg.RegistrationAccessToken))
                {
                    <div>registration_access_token</div><div><code>@reg.RegistrationAccessToken</code></div>
                }
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(reg.RegistrationClientUri))
                {
                    <div>registration_client_uri</div><div><code>@reg.RegistrationClientUri</code></div>
                }
            </div>
            <div class="note warn">
                <strong>Copy the secret and the registration access token now.</strong> Both are
                shown exactly once — the server keeps only hashes. The registration access token is
                not the client's access token and cannot be re-derived: lose it and this
                registration can only be removed by an operator with database access.
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="note bad">
                <strong>@(reg.StatusCode == 0 ? "Request failed" : reg.StatusCode.ToString()) — @reg.Error</strong><br />
                @reg.ErrorDescription
                @if (reg.Error == "invalid_client")
                {
                    <div style="margin-top:6px">
                        The initial access token was missing or invalid — see Step 1.
                    </div>
                }
                else if (reg.Error == "insufficient_scope")
                {
                    <div style="margin-top:6px">
                        The initial access token did not carry the <code>client.register</code>
                        scope. Add the scope to <code>@Model.MachineClientId</code> in the admin
                        console.
                    </div>
                }
                else if (reg.Error == "invalid_redirect_uri")
                {
                    <div style="margin-top:6px">
                        Every redirect URI must be absolute, must use <code>https</code> unless it
                        is a loopback address, and must not contain a fragment.
                    </div>
                }
                else if (reg.Error == "invalid_client_metadata")
                {
                    <div style="margin-top:6px">
                        Usually a <code>client_id</code> or <code>client_name</code> that is already
                        taken, or a scope the IdP's catalogue does not define. The description above
                        says which. A taken id can be removed from the note at the top of Step 2.
                    </div>
                }
                else if (reg.Error == "registration_not_supported")
                {
                    <div style="margin-top:6px">
                        Dynamic registration is switched off on this provider — see the top of this
                        page.
                    </div>
                }
            </div>
        }

        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(reg.RequestBody))
        {
            <h2 style="margin-top:22px">What was sent</h2>
            <pre>@reg.RequestBody</pre>
        }
        <h2 style="margin-top:22px">What came back</h2>
        <pre>@reg.RawResponse</pre>
    </div>
}

<h2 id="step-3">Step 3 — Grant a user access to the client <span class="pill">Ark</span></h2>
<div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
        Message 5. OAuth has no step for this, and Ark cannot do without it: a user with no
        mapping to <code>@clientIdShown</code> cannot sign in to it at all, and the sign-in page
        reports only that the credentials were not recognised.
    </p>
    <form method="post" action="/flows/register/map" data-carry>
        @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
        @{ CarryFields(includeTarget: true, includeManage: true); }
        <div class="kv" style="margin-bottom:14px">
            <div>client</div>
            <div>
                <code>@clientIdShown</code>
                @if (targetExists) { <span class="pill ok">registered</span> }
                else if (Model.TargetLookup == "missing") { <span class="pill warn">not registered — Step 2</span> }
            </div>

            <div>user</div>
            <div>
                <input name="MapUser" value="@Model.MapUser" list="idp-users" style="width:100%"
                       autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" placeholder="the login id to sign in with" />
                <datalist id="idp-users">
                    @foreach (var user in Model.Users)
                    {
                        <option value="@user"></option>
                    }
                </datalist>
            </div>

            <div>display name</div>
            <div>
                <input name="MapDisplayName" value="@Model.MapDisplayName" style="width:100%"
                       autocomplete="off" placeholder="only for a new account — defaults to the user id" />
            </div>

            <div>claims</div>
            <div><input name="MapClaims" value="@Model.MapClaims" style="width:100%" spellcheck="false" /></div>
        </div>
        <button class="btn" type="submit" disabled="@(!secretWorks || !targetExists)">Grant access</button>
        @if (!secretWorks)
        {
            <span class="secret-src">needs a working machine secret — Step 1</span>
        }
        else if (!targetExists)
        {
            <span class="secret-src">register <code>@clientIdShown</code> in Step 2 first</span>
        }
    </form>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
        One call to the provisioning API (<code>POST /api/oauth/v1/provision/user</code>). An
        existing account is left exactly as it is and only mapped; a new one is created with the
        IdP's default password (<code>ark_oauth_server:DefaultPw</code>) and the display name above.
        It writes the same row as <strong>Access mapping</strong> in the
        <a href="@Model.AdminConsoleUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">admin console</a>: claims
        the user already has on this client are kept, <code>sub name email email_verified</code> are
        always included, and the ones above are added.
    </p>

    @if (Model.Mapping != null)
    {
        if (Model.Mapping.Succeeded)
        {
            <div class="note ok">
                <strong><code>@Model.MapUser</code> can now sign in to <code>@clientIdShown</code></strong>
                with <code>@string.Join(" ", Model.MappedClaims)</code>.
                @if (Model.MapUserCredential == "default_password")
                {
                    <text>The account was created and signs in with the IdP's default password.</text>
                }
                else if (Model.MapUserCredential == "activation_email")
                {
                    <text>The account was created and an activation link emailed; it cannot sign in until the link is used.</text>
                }
                else
                {
                    <text>The account already existed and was not changed.</text>
                }
                Claims are written into tokens when they are issued, so sign out and back in (Step 4)
                to see them.
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="note bad">
                <strong>@(Model.Mapping.StatusCode == 0 ? "Not sent" : Model.Mapping.StatusCode.ToString()) — @(Model.Mapping.Code ?? "error")</strong><br />
                @Model.Mapping.Message
            </div>
        }
    }

    @if (Model.AppRequiresArkClaims)
    {
        <div class="note warn">
            <strong>The claim is required here, not optional.</strong> This app has
            <code>AccountSwitch:RequireArkClaims</code> on, so a mapping with no authorization claim
            is refused at the callback and lands on <code>/ark/no-access</code>. Identity claims
            (<code>name</code>, <code>email</code>) do not count.
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="note">
            Identity claims (<code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, …) shape the ID token; any other
            claim is an authorization claim, delivered as <code>ark_claims</code> in the access token.
            Changes take effect at the next sign-in.
        </div>
    }
</div>

<h2 id="step-4">Step 4 — Point this app at the client <span class="pill">config</span></h2>
<div class="card">
    @if (commands.Count == 0)
    {
        <p style="margin-top:0">
            Message 6. This app already signs in as <code>@Model.AppClientId</code>, which is the client
            registered above, and as the same kind of client
            (<strong>@(Model.AppIsConfidential ? "confidential" : "public")</strong>).
        </p>
        <div class="note ok">
            <strong>Nothing to configure and nothing to restart.</strong> The OpenID Connect handler
            was configured with this client id at start-up, and the IdP now knows it.
        </div>
        <p>
            If you are already signed in, sign out once: the session cookie was written before the
            mapping in Step 3, so it does not carry the claims granted there.
        </p>
        @if (signedIn)
        {
            <form method="post" action="/account/logout">
                @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
                <button class="btn ghost" type="submit">Sign out @signedInAs</button>
            </form>
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="pill ok">You are signed out</span>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <p style="margin-top:0">
            Message 6. This app is signing in as <code>@Model.AppClientId</code> right now
            @if (isCurrentApp)
            {
                <text>— the same client id, but registered as a <strong>@(newIsConfidential ? "confidential" : "public")</strong> client while this app is configured as a <strong>@(Model.AppIsConfidential ? "confidential" : "public")</strong> one.</text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>. The authority stays the same; only the client changes.</text>
            }
        </p>
        <ol class="steps">
            <li>
                <strong>Sign out first, while the app still runs as the old client.</strong>
                The session cookie is not tied to a client id, so after the switch it would open
                <code>/home/secure</code> without a new sign-in and you would see nothing happen.
                Signing out now also ends the IdP session cleanly, with an ID token the IdP recognises.
                @if (signedIn)
                {
                    <form method="post" action="/account/logout" style="margin-top:8px">
                        @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
                        <button class="btn ghost" type="submit">Sign out @signedInAs</button>
                    </form>
                }
                else
                {
                    <div style="margin-top:6px"><span class="pill ok">You are signed out</span></div>
                }
            </li>
            <li>
                Change the configuration with user-secrets, from the <code>Ark.oAuth.Oidc</code> folder.
                Nothing goes into <code>appsettings.json</code>, so reverting is one command.
                <pre style="margin-top:8px">@switchCommands</pre>
            </li>
            <li>
                Restart this app. The client is read once at start-up, when the OpenID Connect
                handler is configured — changing it while the app runs has no effect.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <div class="note">
            This is the same configuration a brand-new application would carry: an
            <code>Authority</code> of <code>@Model.Authority</code> and the
            <code>ClientId</code>. Endpoints and signing keys are discovered, so nothing else changes.
        </div>
    }
</div>

<h2 id="step-5">Step 5 — Open the protected page <span class="pill">RFC 6749 §4.1 + PKCE</span></h2>
<div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
        Messages 7–14. Open <code>/home/secure</code> in this browser. It carries
        <code>[Authorize]</code>, so the request starts the whole sign-in by itself:
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Authorization request.</strong> With no cookie, the OIDC handler redirects to
            the IdP's <code>/oauth2/authorize</code> with <code>client_id=@clientIdShown</code>, a
            <code>code_challenge</code>, a <code>state</code> and a <code>nonce</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Authentication.</strong> The IdP checks the client and redirect URI, shows its
            sign-in form, and checks the user's mapping to the client. Sign in as the user from
            Step 3.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Authorization response.</strong> The IdP redirects back to
            <code>@Model.AppRedirectUri</code> with a one-time <code>code</code>; the handler checks
            the <code>state</code> matches what it sent.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Token exchange.</strong> The handler posts the code and its
            <code>code_verifier</code> to the token endpoint, receives the tokens, and validates the
            ID token's signature, issuer, audience (<code>@clientIdShown</code>) and nonce.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Session.</strong> It writes the encrypted cookie and returns you to
            <code>/home/secure</code>.
        </li>
    </ol>
    <a class="btn" href="/home/secure">Open the protected page</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="/home/profile">Open my profile</a>
    <div class="note ok">
        <strong>How to tell it worked:</strong> the profile page shows <code>aud</code> and
        <code>client_id</code> as <code>@clientIdShown</code> in both tokens, and the claim you
        mapped in Step 3 in <code>ark_claims</code>.
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="troubleshooting">When a step fails</h2>
<div class="card">
    <div class="scroll-x">
        <table>
            <tr><th>What you see</th><th>Why</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The IdP's sign-in page says the credentials were not recognised, and they are right</td>
                <td>The user has no access mapping to the client</td>
                <td><a class="step" href="#step-3">Step 3</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Signed in, but sent to <code>/ark/no-access</code></td>
                <td>The mapping exists but carries no authorization claim</td>
                <td>Add a claim in <a class="step" href="#step-3">Step 3</a>, then use <strong>Sign in as a different user</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The IdP shows an error about <code>redirect_uri</code></td>
                <td>The registered URI differs from what this app sends — port, scheme or trailing slash</td>
                <td>Register again with exactly <code>@Model.AppRedirectUri</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The IdP answers <code>invalid_client</code> at sign-in</td>
                <td>The app is still running with the old id, the id has a typo, or the registration was deleted</td>
                <td>Check <a class="step" href="#step-4">Step 4</a> and restart the app</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The IdP answers <code>invalid_scope</code></td>
                <td>This app requests a scope the new client was not registered for</td>
                <td>Register with every scope in <code>ark_oauth_client:Scopes</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>The protected page opens with no sign-in at all</td>
                <td>The old session cookie is still there</td>
                <td>Sign out, or use a private window</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Back on the Setup page with an <code>auth_error</code> after signing in</td>
                <td>The token exchange failed — usually a confidential client with no secret configured, or a public one with a secret left over</td>
                <td>Match <code>ClientSecret</code> to the registration's <code>token_endpoint_auth_method</code></td>
            </tr>
        </table>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="step-6">Step 6 — Manage or undo the registration <span class="pill">RFC 7592</span></h2>
<div class="card">
    <p style="margin-top:0">
        The registration access token authenticates these calls, and nothing else does — not the
        client's own access token, not the initial access token that created it.
    </p>
    <form method="post" action="/flows/register/manage" data-carry>
        @Html.AntiForgeryToken()
        @{ CarryFields(includeTarget: true, includeManage: false); }
        <div class="kv" style="margin-bottom:14px">
            <div>client_id</div>
            <div><input name="clientId" value="@Model.ManageClientId" style="width:100%" /></div>
            <div>registration_access_token</div>
            <div><input name="accessToken" value="@Model.ManageAccessToken" style="width:100%" /></div>
        </div>
        <button class="btn" type="submit" name="action" value="read">Read registration</button>
        <button class="btn ghost" type="submit" name="action" value="delete">Delete registration</button>
    </form>
    @if (isCurrentApp)
    {
        <div class="note warn">
            <strong><code>@clientIdShown</code> is this app's own registration.</strong> Delete it and
            this app goes on running as a client that no longer exists: every sign-in fails with
            <code>invalid_client</code> until it is registered again in Step 2. The token is only
            on this page until it is reloaded; after that, remove the client from the note in Step 2.
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="note">
            <strong>To put this app back the way it was:</strong> sign out, remove the override and
            restart, then delete the registration here.
            <pre style="margin-top:8px">dotnet user-secrets remove "ark_oauth_client:ClientId" --project Ark.Client.Web</pre>
            Keep that order. Delete the registration first and the app goes on running as a client
            that no longer exists, so every sign-in fails with <code>invalid_client</code> until the
            override is removed.
        </div>
    }
</div>

@if (Model.Management != null)
{
    var mgmt = Model.Management;

    <h2>@(Model.ManagementAction == "delete" ? "Delete" : "Read") result</h2>
    <div class="card">
        @if (mgmt.Succeeded)
        {
            <div class="note ok">
                <strong>@mgmt.StatusCode — @(Model.ManagementAction == "delete" ? "registration deleted." : "registration read.")</strong>
                @if (Model.ManagementAction == "delete")
                {
                    <text>
                        The client no longer exists. Any token it was issued stays valid until it
                        expires — deleting a registration does not revoke tokens already out there.
                    </text>
                }
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <div class="note bad">
                <strong>@(mgmt.StatusCode == 0 ? "Request failed" : mgmt.StatusCode.ToString()) — @mgmt.Error</strong><br />
                @mgmt.ErrorDescription
            </div>
        }
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(mgmt.RawResponse))
        {
            <pre>@mgmt.RawResponse</pre>
        }
    </div>
}

<h2>The code</h2>
<div class="card">
    <pre>// Both steps come from Ark.oAuth.Client. The registration endpoint is read from
// discovery: if the provider does not advertise one, registration is disabled.
var initial = await credentials.GetTokenAsync(machineId, machineSecret, new[] { "client.register" });

var metadata = new JsonObject
